Follow-up to aiecompiler_hostlib_missing.log, which found no host C++ standard
library anywhere on this machine (no Visual Studio install, nothing bundled
in the ryzen-ai-1.7.1 pip env). Installed Visual Studio 2022 Build Tools
with the C++ (VCTools) workload via winget:

  winget install --id Microsoft.VisualStudio.2022.BuildTools --silent \
    --accept-package-agreements --accept-source-agreements --override \
    "--add Microsoft.VisualStudio.Workload.VCTools --includeRecommended --passive --norestart"

That installed MSVC 14.44.35207 (VC/Tools/MSVC/14.44.35207/include, has
iostream and the rest of the STL) and pulled in a Windows 10 SDK,
10.0.26100.0 (Windows Kits/10/Include/10.0.26100.0/{ucrt,shared,um}), even
though this machine runs Windows 11 build 26200 -- the two numbers are
independent (SDK version vs. OS build), not a mismatch to worry about.

Passed all three as extra --include paths to aiecompiler (it accepts the
flag repeated, accumulating a search list) alongside the existing
vaie_cpplus include:

  --include=<vaie_cpplus>/include
  --include=<VS Build Tools>/VC/Tools/MSVC/14.44.35207/include
  --include=<Windows Kits>/10/Include/10.0.26100.0/ucrt
  --include=<Windows Kits>/10/Include/10.0.26100.0/shared
  --include=<Windows Kits>/10/Include/10.0.26100.0/um

Result: the `<iostream>` failure is completely gone. The ADF frontend's own
preprocessing pass succeeds, and the pipeline advances much further than
ever before in this investigation -- through a "frontend executable" build
(graph.exe, an MSVC-style link of the graph description against
aiecompiler_client.lib) and into real graph analysis.

Along the way, the pre-existing trivial graph in tools/aie_probe/graph.h hit
its own, unrelated bug: `connect<>(...)` with an empty template parameter
list is rejected for window ports ("The template parameter cannot be empty
when connecting a kernel port... specified based on the kernel port type").
Fixed by giving both connect<> calls an explicit adf::window<32> argument
(our kernel's window::readincr loop processes 32 int8 elements = 32 bytes;
`window<SIZE, MARGIN=0>` is defined in adf/new_frontend/types.h). That
cleared both errors -- confirmed no code-level issue remains in the graph
description itself.

With that fixed, the compiler reads our graph, resolves the derived device,
and prints:

  INFO: [aiecompiler 77-749] Reading logical device aie2_5x4_device

-- twice, once during a validation pass and once for the real run. This is
the strongest confirmation yet that xc10AIE24x5-die-1LP-e-S-es1 (see
aiecompiler_part_phoenix_candidate.log) is genuinely Phoenix: "aie2_5x4"
names both the architecture (AIE2) and the exact 5-column-x-4-row physical
layout the pre-existing xrt-smi measurement in docs/DECISIONS.md already
established for this chip, independently of this investigation.

New wall, one level past device derivation:

  ERROR: [aiecompiler 77-22139] Could not find the .../lib/win64.o/
  physical_device.dll file

Checked whether this file exists anywhere -- not just the ryzen-ai-1.7.1
pip env, the whole C: drive (`Get-ChildItem -Recurse -Filter
physical_device.dll` from the root): zero matches, anywhere on this
machine. "Logical device" (the architecture/topology description, aie2_5x4
in this case) resolved fine from data already present; "physical device"
(presumably real place-and-route/timing data for actual silicon) is a
missing file, not a missing environment variable or include path -- nothing
here can supply it. This SDK's `vaie-overlay`/`vaie-cpplus` packaging was
very plausibly built to support the ORT/VitisAI EP's own lightweight
JIT-compile flow (pre-built xclbins, no full place-and-route needed), not
the classical AIE compiler's real hardware-target path -- consistent with
this whole investigation's starting premise, that nothing in this repo
(or apparently in this pip distribution) uses `--target=hw` for real.

Not attempted: hunting for physical_device.dll in a different AMD
distribution (a full Vitis/Vivado install, or a different Ryzen AI SDK
version) -- that would mean acquiring and installing an entirely separate,
much larger toolchain on spec, a bigger commitment than this pass's
== install Build Tools, try the fix == scope.

